> it seems like the _store is only ever initialized with a Stream that's under our control, right?

@stephentoub I don't think that's correct. There is a public `ResourceReader(Stream)` constructor that populates `_store` with the user `Stream`. Of course most users are probably going through `ResourceManager` / `RuntimeResourceSet` where the runtime owns the stream. 

However, when it comes to calling arbitrary user code while holding the lock, this class is _already doing that_ because locking on `this` is `ResourceReader`'s primary existing synchronization mechanism. For example, consider the following code:
```
void Main()
{
	var myStream = new MyStream();
	var reader = new ResourceReader(myStream);
	myStream.Reader = reader;
	// prints "Calling user code from within locked ResourceReader" many times
	foreach (DictionaryEntry entry in reader) { }
}

class MyStream : MemoryStream
{
	public ResourceReader Reader { get; set; }
	
	public MyStream() : base(GetBytes()) { }
	
	private static byte[] GetBytes()
	{
		using var stream = typeof(Expression).Assembly.GetManifestResourceStream("FxResources.System.Linq.Expressions.SR.resources");
		var bytes = new byte[stream.Length];
		if (stream.Read(bytes, 0, bytes.Length) != bytes.Length) { throw new Exception(); }
		return bytes;
	}
	
	public override int Read(byte[] buffer, int offset, int count)
	{
		if (this.Reader != null && Monitor.IsEntered(this.Reader)) 
		{ 
			Console.WriteLine("Calling user code from within locked ResourceReader");
		}
		return base.Read(buffer, offset, count);
	}
}
```

